  tools   : tools/snmpq.py on tb277 runs each net-snmp query and classifies it as
            VALUE / NOSUCH / DENIED / AUTHFAIL / TIMEOUT / ERROR, grading it against
            `--expect` (net-snmp returns exit 1 for most of those, so a raw exit code
            cannot grade a step); tools/snmpwire.py decodes the pcap with no dependencies
            and reports per SNMP message the version, msgFlags, USM user, engine ID and
            whether msgData is a plaintext scopedPDU or an encryptedPDU; tools/ckcase.py
            pulled the authored case text from ck.db; tools/ckcon.py and tools/cfg.py
            drove the console, tools/ckyn.py answered the `(y/n)` prompts and
            tools/ckreload.py drove the reload; tools/qmark.py ran the read-only `?`
            probes; tools/rc2cfg.py produced swi_d.cfg; all of them through the tools/tb
            wrapper on the box.

  Method: this is a request/response protocol, so every counted leg carries BOTH flows --
  the OID the view must permit and the OID the view must refuse, read by the SAME user
  against the SAME engine with the SAME credentials in the same leg -- and no verdict
  rests on one of them alone. Every leg also carries an INSTRUMENT CONTROL (an ICMP
  exchange over the same link, or a read that must succeed) so that a refusal is a
  measurement and not a dead path, a mangled credential or a locked-out user. The case's
  passwords are 20 characters of shell-live punctuation (`!#$%&'()*+,-./[\]^_`` and
  `:;<=@{}~0123456789ab`); the host-side commands below print a non-disclosing
  fingerprint, `<PASS len=20 sha1=0eeef6a537>` for auth and `<PASS len=20 sha1=a587e0b9da>`
  for priv, and those two fingerprints are identical in every leg, so the client used the
  same bytes throughout. The decisive proof that nothing was eaten in transit is
  behavioural: an authPriv request the agent ANSWERS WITH A VALUE has already passed the
  MD5 digest check and the DES decryption at the agent, which a mangled password cannot
  produce. What no `show` command can establish -- whether the varbinds are really
  encrypted in flight -- is taken off the wire in STEP 11.

