STEP 5A -- GetNext at the MIB-view boundary gets no response (finding F-12)  done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a walk that times out could be a broken instrument, a throttled agent or a
  product defect. Four controlled probes separate them, the two silences bracketed by
  reads that must succeed.

box$ /usr/bin/snmpget -On -t 3 -r 1 -v 3 -u TESTtestTESTtestTEST -l authPriv -a MD5 \
       -A <PASS len=20 sha1=0eeef6a537> -x DES -X <PASS len=20 sha1=a587e0b9da> \
       10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0
  | .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus
  RESULT VALUE -- GRADE PASS -- expected VALUE, got VALUE        (instrument alive)

box$ /usr/bin/snmpgetnext -On -t 5.0 -r 2 -v 3 -u TESTtestTESTtestTEST -l authPriv ... \
       10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.9.1.4.8      <-- the LAST in-view OID; its lexical
  | Timeout: No Response from 10.38.22.34.        successor falls outside the view
  RESULT TIMEOUT    rc=4

box$ /usr/bin/snmpgetnext -On -t 5.0 -r 2 -v 3 ... 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1.0
  | .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.2.0 = OID: .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.1.14.204
  RESULT VALUE      rc=0                     <-- a GetNext INSIDE the view is answered,
                                                 in milliseconds, between the silences

box$ /usr/bin/snmpgetnext -On -t 5.0 -r 3 -v 3 ... 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.2.1
  | Timeout: No Response from 10.38.22.34.
  RESULT TIMEOUT    rc=4                     <-- 5 s timeout, 3 retries, still nothing

box$ /usr/bin/snmpget -On -t 3 -r 1 -v 3 ... 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0
  | .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus
  RESULT VALUE -- GRADE PASS                 (still alive after both silences)

  => STEP 5A done, and recorded as finding F-12 (PRODUCT): an SNMPv3 GetNext whose
     successor OID falls outside the requesting user's MIB view receives NO RESPONSE AT
     ALL from this build. RFC 3416 §4.2.2 requires the varbind to be returned with the
     value endOfMibView. It is not slowness, throttling or a dead agent: an in-view
     GetNext is answered in milliseconds between the two silences, and a permitted GET
     succeeds immediately before and immediately after, with 5 s timeouts and up to 3
     retries on the silent probes. A GET of an out-of-view OID is handled correctly
     (noSuchObject) -- only GetNext is silent. Operator-visible consequence: `snmpwalk`
     against any view-restricted SNMPv3 user cannot terminate cleanly on this build; it
     stalls at the view boundary and ends in a client timeout, which reads as an
     unreachable device.

