STEP 10 -- beyond the authored procedure: do authentication and privacy actually bite?  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: one attempt per limb (`--retries 0`), because the agent locks a user out for
  300 s after 5 authentication failures. Four failures were spent in total, under the
  threshold.

  E3a  authNoPriv request from a user whose group requires priv, CORRECT auth password:
box$ snmpget ... -l authNoPriv -a MD5 -A <PASS len=20 sha1=0eeef6a537> 10.38.22.34 ...
  | Error in packet
  | Reason: authorizationError (access denied to that object)
  RESULT DENIED                          <-- the PRIVACY requirement bites: same user,
                                             same correct auth password, one notch down
                                             in security level, and the read is refused

  E3b  WRONG auth password (20 chars, 'WRONGwrongWRONGwrong'), correct priv password:
  | snmpget: Authentication failure (incorrect password, community or key)
  RESULT AUTHFAIL                        <-- the AUTHENTICATION requirement bites

  E3c  correct auth password, WRONG priv password:
  | Timeout: No Response from 10.38.22.34.
  RESULT TIMEOUT                         <-- denied, but SILENTLY. RFC 3414 §3.2(8)
                                             expects usmStatsDecryptionErrors and a
                                             report PDU. The access outcome is correct
                                             and this is a common implementation choice,
                                             so it is recorded as observation O-12, in
                                             the same silence family as F-12

  E3d  unknown user name 'NOSUCHUSER':
  | snmpget: Unknown user name
  RESULT AUTHFAIL

  E3e  INSTRUMENT CONTROL, immediately after the failures:
  | .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1.0 = STRING: Allied Telesis router/switch, Software (AlliedWare Plus) Version sin_x96056-20261012-24
  RESULT VALUE      GRADE PASS

  => STEP 10 PASS: each of the three security properties is refused when its condition is
     broken and granted when it is not -- privacy, authentication and user identity -- and
     the closing control proves the user is not locked out and the correct credential
     still works, so every refusal is attributable to the one thing that was changed and
     to nothing else.

