  #17 +37.273s 10.38.22.33:35354 -> 10.38.22.34:161 SNMPv3 msgID=826779854 flags=a-r user='TESTtestTESTtestTEST' engineID=80001f88...  msgData=PLAINTEXT scopedPDU pdu=GetRequest
        varbind OIDs in the clear: 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1.0
  #18 +37.273s 10.38.22.34:161 -> 10.38.22.33:35354 SNMPv3 msgID=826779854 flags=a-- user='TESTtestTESTtestTEST' engineID=80001f88...  msgData=PLAINTEXT scopedPDU pdu=Response
        varbind OIDs in the clear: 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1.0
  SUMMARY 18 SNMP message(s): 6 ENCRYPTED, 12 PLAINTEXT

  Reading, and the controls are what make it sound:
    - the six authPriv messages (#3/#4, #7/#8, #11/#12) carry msgFlags auth+priv and
      their msgData is an ASN.1 OCTET STRING, an encryptedPDU. No OID and no value is
      visible in any of them;
    - #17/#18 are the IN-CAPTURE CONTROL: same user, same engine, same auth password, one
      notch down to authNoPriv -- and msgData is a PLAINTEXT scopedPDU with
      1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1.0 legible on the wire. The decoder CAN see varbinds in this traffic
      when they are there; it saw none in the priv messages because there are none to
      see. The encryption is real, not a decoder artifact;
    - #13/#14 are a second control from another angle: SNMPv2c puts community='public'
      and the OID in the clear, exactly as expected;
    - #1/#2 and #15/#16 are the RFC 3414 engine-discovery exchange, correctly
      noAuthNoPriv and plaintext, carrying usmStatsUnknownEngineIDs
      (1.3.6.1.6.3.15.1.1.4.0). The engine ID the DUT puts on the wire,
      80001f88808e3cab4244f4d83800000000, is byte-identical to the one `show snmp-server`
      prints.
    - all 18 packets are between 10.38.22.33 and 10.38.22.34; the third station on the
      segment contributed nothing to this window.

  => STEP 11 PASS: authPriv is not merely configured, it is honoured on the wire -- the
     varbinds of every priv message are encrypted and unreadable, while an authNoPriv
     message from the same user in the same capture exposes its OID in the clear. The
     control is what proves it: the measurement would be worthless if the decoder simply
     could not find varbinds, and it found them whenever they were present.

